              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 6.37: The slices can be rearranged to form a parallelogram-like structure"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.505,
            0.885,
            0.565
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As the slices become smaller and smaller, the arcs in Fig. 6.37B become more and more closer to a line. This makes the figure more and more closer to a parallelogram with"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.268,
            0.569,
            0.729,
            0.59
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "base = half the circumference (why?) = πr,"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.269,
            0.594,
            0.461,
            0.615
          ],
          "image_path": "images/page_28_equation_11.jpg",
          "content": "\\mathrm{height} = \\mathrm{radius} r."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.619,
            0.884,
            0.659
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "This gives a way to argue that the area of the circle is equal to the area of a parallelogram!"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.662,
            0.868,
            0.684
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Area of the circle = Area of the parallelogram = base × height = πr"
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "2",
              "styles": [
                "superscript"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.707,
            0.516,
            0.728
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "6.10.1 Area of Sector of a Circle"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.736,
            0.61,
            0.795
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A sector of a circle is the region bounded by an arc and the two radii containing the endpoints of the arc."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.795,
            0.612,
            0.873
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "The area within a sector of a circle may be found the same way that we found the length of a circular arc. Examine Fig. 6.39 and Fig. 6.40."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
